---
import {
  ChartUpIcon,
  Copy01Icon,
  Key01Icon,
  Robot02Icon,
  Tick02Icon,
  Wallet01Icon,
} from "@hugeicons/core-free-icons";
import { ico } from "../lib/appicon";
import PointerCursor from "./PointerCursor.astro";

/**
 * Step 01's visual: signing in to AgentPit and walking away with a key.
 *
 * ── What this is drawn from ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 * AgentPit's real screens (owner reference shots, 2026-08-18), simplified.
 * Three of them, in the order a reader will meet them:
 *
 *   1. the sign-in modal   - "Sign in", an email field, a navy "Send code"
 *   2. the navy key pill   - a key glyph and "Your key is ready"
 *   3. the key card        - a blue-tinted panel, "funded & ready" on the
 *                            right, a wallet glyph, the key, and Copy
 *
 * SIMPLIFIED, not transcribed. The homepage cards copy our own screens
 * component by component; these are someone else's, so this takes the palette,
 * the shapes and the distinctive strings and leaves the rest. What it must not
 * do is drift into inventing AgentPit UI that does not exist.
 *
 * Two deliberate departures from the reference:
 *
 *  - SENTENCE CASE, where their labels are letter-spaced caps ("YOUR API KEY",
 *    "EMAIL"). The house rule is that nothing in our UI is all-caps and that
 *    size, weight and colour carry hierarchy instead. Fidelity to a partner's
 *    screen does not outrank it in a drawing that lives on our page.
 *  - A DIFFERENT UUID. The reference shot shows what is plausibly a live key
 *    from a real account; it is not reproduced here. This one is invented and
 *    should stay invented.
 *
 * The sign-in modal drops their "OR / Continue with Google" half. At this size
 * the two buttons and the divider between them stop being legible and start
 * being texture, and the beat only needs one thing to click.
 *
 * ── The window ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * ReefVisual's construction (owner, 2026-08-18): a frosted panel set INTO the
 * card rather than drawn on it - inset from the top and sides, bleeding off
 * the bottom edge, with a rim highlight and a shadow tinted to the panel it
 * sits in. The bleed is what sells it as a window onto something larger; a box
 * closed on all four sides would just be a card inside a card.
 *
 * It bleeds DOWNWARD only, unlike Reef's, which also runs off to the right.
 * Reef's content is a grid where any corner tells the story; this scene has
 * things that must be clickable on screen, and a right-hand bleed puts the
 * Copy button past the edge.
 *
 * ── The loop ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * 9s, CSS only, held at its 0% frame until PlayOnView marks the scene (12s
 * originally; quickened on the owner's call, 2026-08-18). The percentages
 * below are the choreography and are unchanged - the duration is the only
 * knob, and every element shares it, so they cannot drift out of step.
 *
 * Three clicks, one per screen:
 *
 *   04-20%  cursor arrives, clicks "Send code"          -> signed in
 *   22-44%  cursor clicks "Your key is ready"           -> the key card
 *   46-68%  cursor clicks Copy                          -> tick
 *   68-86%  hold on the result
 *   86-100% the seam
 *
 * SEAM, matching the homepage cards exactly: dissolve at 86-89%, put
 * everything back at 89-94% while nothing is visible, fade in by 96.8%. 0% and
 * 100% are then the same frame and the wrap cannot be seen.
 *
 * ── Rules this file follows ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  - NO `style` attributes. The production CSP drops them, so every position
 *    below is a class in the stylesheet (see AppWindow.astro).
 *  - Settled by DEFAULT. The base rules draw the finished state - key card up,
 *    no cursor - so reduced motion and the moment before PlayOnView both show
 *    a sensible still. The 0% state is declared inside the no-preference
 *    query, which is the only place the animation exists.
 *  - `--u` and container units, not viewport units, so the scene scales with
 *    its own panel. The panel is half a rail on desktop and full width on a
 *    phone; anything sized off `vw` breaks across that.
 *  - Compositor-only motion: the cursor moves by translating a wrapper the
 *    size of the whole scene, so a percentage is a percentage OF THE SCENE and
 *    nothing has to know its own pixel position.
 */
---

<div class="scene" data-play-on-view>
  <div class="win">
    <header class="whead">
      <img class="wmark" src="/brand/agentpit.svg" alt="" width="24" height="24" />
      <span class="wname">AgentPit</span>
      <span class="wbeta">Beta</span>
      {/* The real nav, now that the reference shows it: two outlined pills,
          each with its glyph. The blank pills that stood here were a guess and
          are gone. */}
      <span class="wnav">
        <span class="wpill">
          <span class="pico" set:html={ico(ChartUpIcon)} />Markets
        </span>
        <span class="wpill">
          <span class="pico" set:html={ico(Robot02Icon)} />Arena
        </span>
      </span>
    </header>

    {/* All three screens share ONE grid cell, so the window's height never
        changes as they swap. Content that grew mid-loop would move the scene
        under a reader mid-scroll - the same class of problem
        `overflow-anchor` is turned off for below. */}
    <div class="wbody">
      {/* ── 1. Sign in ── */}
      <span class="stage s-signin">
        <span class="s-title">Sign in</span>
        <span class="s-label">Email</span>
        <span class="s-input"></span>
        <span class="btn btn-navy b-send">Send code</span>
        {/* Restored (owner, 2026-08-18). It was dropped as "texture at this
            size" and that was the wrong call: the divider is what tells you
            the two buttons are alternatives rather than a sequence, and
            Google is the route most readers will actually take - so it is
            what the cursor clicks. */}
        <span class="s-or"><i></i>or<i></i></span>
        <span class="btn btn-ghost b-google">
          Continue with Google
          <span class="ring r1"></span>
        </span>
      </span>

      {/* ── 2. The key is minted, and the landing page says so ──
          NOT a lone pill on an empty screen, which is what this was before the
          reference arrived: the button lives in AgentPit's hero, and the hero
          is most of what makes the beat recognisable as their site. Eyebrow,
          two-line headline with the second line in their blue, one line of
          body, then the button pair. */}
      <span class="stage s-ready">
        <span class="h-eyebrow">Prediction market sandbox</span>
        <span class="h-title">
          <span class="h-l1">Train in the Sandbox.</span>
          <span class="h-l2">Rule the Leaderboard.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="h-sub">
          Fine-tune your agent on identical Polymarket mechanics, risk free.
        </span>
        <span class="h-cta">
          <span class="btn btn-navy b-ready">
            <span class="bico" set:html={ico(Key01Icon)} />
            Your key is ready
            <span class="ring r2"></span>
          </span>
          {/* Its right edge falls off the window's bleed, which is the point -
              a hero that ended tidily inside the frame would stop reading as a
              page and start reading as a card. */}
          <span class="btn btn-ghost">
            <span class="bico" set:html={ico(ChartUpIcon)} />
            Explore the Pit
          </span>
        </span>
      </span>

      {/* ── 3. The key itself ── */}
      <span class="stage s-key">
        {/* The numeral from AgentPit's own "01 / Get your key" block. It is
            what places this card in a sequence rather than leaving it
            floating - which is exactly what the step it illustrates is doing
            on our page. */}
        <span class="k-num">01</span>
        <span class="k-main">
          <span class="k-title">Get your key</span>
          <span class="kcard">
          <span class="kchead">
            <span class="kctitle">Your API key</span>
            <span class="kcstate">funded &amp; ready</span>
          </span>
          <span class="kcrow">
            <span class="kcwallet" set:html={ico(Wallet01Icon)} />
            <span class="kcval">a41f7d92-6c05-4e8b-9f31-2b7ce0d4a6f8</span>
            <span class="kcopy">
              <span class="kico kc" set:html={ico(Copy01Icon)} />
              <span class="kico kt" set:html={ico(Tick02Icon)} />
              <span class="kcopy-l">Copy</span>
              <span class="ring r3"></span>
            </span>
          </span>
            <span class="kcnote">Your live key, minted for this account.</span>
          </span>
        </span>
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* Two wrappers, two jobs: -track carries the path, -press carries the dip.
      Splitting them keeps each animation on one property, so they compose
      instead of overwriting one another. */}
  <span class="cur-track">
    <span class="cur-press"><PointerCursor /></span>
  </span>
</div>

<style>
  .scene {
    /* Makes `cqw` below resolve against THIS box rather than the viewport. */
    container-type: inline-size;
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    /* Nothing in here is content a reader tracks, so nothing in here should
     * ever be picked as a scroll anchor - Chrome will otherwise "correct" for
     * the scene's own motion by scrolling the page. */
    overflow-anchor: none;

    /* 1u == 1px at the panel's reference width (~500px: the rails' 1.1fr
     * column at the desktop measure). Everything below is a multiple, so the
     * scene scales with ITSELF and not with the window. */
    --u: clamp(0.68px, 0.2cqw, 1.15px);
    /* The window's top and left gap. One value, two edges - see .win. */
    --win-in: calc(var(--u) * 34);

    /* AgentPit's palette, read off the reference shots. */
    --ap-navy: #111827;
    --ap-blue: #2563eb;
    /* Two blues, and they are genuinely different in the reference: the key
     * card's label is the calmer one above, the hero headline is this. */
    --ap-hero: #3b5bfd;
    --ap-ink: #0f172a;
    --ap-mut: #64748b;
    --ap-tint: #f5f8ff;
    --ap-line: #dbe4fb;
    --line: rgb(20 22 26 / 0.09);
  }

  /* ── The window ──
   * Set into the panel, not drawn on it. Reef's offset (owner, 2026-08-18):
   * pushed in from the LEFT and running off the right and the bottom, so the
   * frame crops a window onto something larger rather than centring a card in
   * a box.
   *
   * The right bleed is small where Reef's is -16%, and the difference is not
   * timidity. Reef's content is a grid where any corner tells the story; this
   * scene has a Copy button at the far right of the key card that the third
   * click has to land on. Deepen the bleed and that click goes off the edge -
   * so re-measure the three targets against the rendered scene before touching
   * this, and carry the new numbers into `ap-path`. */
  .win {
    position: absolute;
    /* ONE VALUE for the top and the left gap (owner, 2026-08-18), and it has
     * to be a length rather than a percentage: `inset: 11% ... 16%` reads its
     * top from the panel's HEIGHT and its left from the panel's WIDTH, so on
     * anything but a square the two gaps are different sizes no matter what
     * numbers you write. They were 36px and 79px. In `u` they are the same
     * number because they are the same unit, and they stay the same through
     * any future change to the panel's aspect ratio. */
    top: var(--win-in);
    left: var(--win-in);
    /* The bleeds are free to differ - they are off-frame. */
    right: calc(var(--u) * -20);
    bottom: calc(var(--u) * -66);
    /* ASYMMETRIC, and the right value is the whole point: the window runs 20u
     * past the panel's edge, so a symmetric 16u padding left content ending 4u
     * OUTSIDE the frame - the Copy button was being sliced by the crop. The
     * right padding has to clear the bleed and then leave a gutter, or content
     * touches the edge of the box. Keep it larger than the right bleed. */
    padding: calc(var(--u) * 16) calc(var(--u) * 46) calc(var(--u) * 16)
      calc(var(--u) * 16);
    border-radius: calc(var(--u) * 14);
    background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.88);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
    /* Rim light first, then a shadow tinted to the step's own blue - a neutral
     * grey shadow on a tinted panel reads as dirt under the window. */
    box-shadow:
      inset 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 255 255 / 0.85),
      0 calc(var(--u) * 14) calc(var(--u) * 30) rgb(36 99 235 / 0.16);
  }

  .whead {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 8);
    padding-bottom: calc(var(--u) * 12);
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--u) * 14);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    /* The rule runs to the WINDOW's edge, not the content's (owner,
     * 2026-08-18). The window carries 46u of right padding to clear its own
     * bleed, which stopped this line well short and made the header look like
     * a floating card rather than a bar. Cancelling that padding with a
     * negative margin and re-adding it as padding here lets the border span
     * the full width while the nav pills stay exactly where they were. */
    margin-inline-end: calc(var(--u) * -46);
    padding-inline-end: calc(var(--u) * 46);
  }

  .wmark {
    width: calc(var(--u) * 18);
    height: calc(var(--u) * 18);
  }

  .wname {
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 15);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--ap-ink);
  }

  /* Their beta badge. Orange is the only warm note in AgentPit's palette and
   * it is what the eye finds first in the reference shot, so it earns its
   * place even at this size. */
  .wbeta {
    padding: calc(var(--u) * 2) calc(var(--u) * 6);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: #f97316;
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 9);
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .wnav {
    display: flex;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 7);
    margin-inline-start: calc(var(--u) * 14);
  }

  .wpill {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 5);
    height: calc(var(--u) * 22);
    padding-inline: calc(var(--u) * 10);
    border: 1px solid var(--ap-line);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 11);
    color: var(--ap-mut);
  }

  .pico {
    display: block;
    width: calc(var(--u) * 12);
    height: calc(var(--u) * 12);
  }

  /* ── The hero ── */
  .h-eyebrow {
    /* The hero starts lower than the modal does - a headline that begins
     * immediately under the nav reads as cramped where a form does not. */
    margin-top: calc(var(--u) * 14);
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 9.5);
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: var(--ap-hero);
  }

  .h-title {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 25);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 1.12;
  }

  .h-l1 {
    color: var(--ap-ink);
  }

  /* The second line in their blue - the one detail that makes the headline
   * theirs rather than any hero on any site. */
  .h-l2 {
    color: var(--ap-hero);
  }

  .h-sub {
    max-width: calc(var(--u) * 300);
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 11);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ap-mut);
  }

  .h-cta {
    display: flex;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 9);
    margin-top: calc(var(--u) * 2);
  }

  /* Shorter and self-sizing in the hero - the full-width 38u button is the
   * modal's shape, not this one's. */
  .h-cta .btn {
    flex: none;
    height: calc(var(--u) * 34);
    padding-inline: calc(var(--u) * 14);
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 12.5);
  }

  .btn-ghost {
    border: 1px solid var(--ap-line);
    background: #fff;
    color: var(--ap-ink);
  }

  /* ── The three screens, stacked in one cell ── */
  .wbody {
    display: grid;
  }

  .stage {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* The shorter screens centre in the cell the tallest one sizes. */
    justify-content: center;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 8);
  }

  /* THE SETTLED FRAME, and it has to be declared: three screens sharing one
   * cell are all visible at once unless something says otherwise, and the
   * no-preference block below is not that something - a reduced-motion visitor
   * never reads it. Without these two rules that visitor gets the sign-in
   * modal, the pill and the key card printed on top of each other.
   *
   * The end of the story is the right still: the key, already minted. */
  .s-signin,
  .s-ready {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .s-key {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* The hero's own spacing - looser between the headline block and what
   * frames it than the modal's uniform 8u. */
  .s-ready {
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 10);
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .s-title {
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 17);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--ap-ink);
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--u) * 4);
  }

  .s-label {
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 11);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--ap-mut);
  }

  .s-input {
    height: calc(var(--u) * 34);
    border: 1px solid var(--ap-line);
    border-radius: calc(var(--u) * 8);
    background: #fff;
  }

  /* The alternatives divider: a rule, the word, a rule. */
  .s-or {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 10);
    margin: calc(var(--u) * 2) 0;
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 10.5);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--ap-mut);
  }

  .s-or i {
    flex: 1;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--ap-line);
  }

  /* ── The key beat ── */
  .s-key {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 16);
    /* Same air the hero's eyebrow takes: a numbered block starting flush
     * under the nav divider reads as attached to the chrome above it rather
     * than as the start of its own section. */
    margin-top: calc(var(--u) * 16);
  }

  /* Big, pale and set back - an index, not a heading. Their own numeral is
   * the lightest thing in the block and it still reads first because of the
   * size, which is the trick worth copying. */
  .k-num {
    flex: none;
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 30);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: #bcd0f8;
  }

  .k-main {
    display: flex;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 9);
  }

  .k-title {
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 15);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--ap-ink);
  }

  /* ── Buttons ── */
  .btn {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 8);
    height: calc(var(--u) * 38);
    border-radius: calc(var(--u) * 9);
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 14);
    font-weight: 550;
  }

  .btn-navy {
    background: var(--ap-navy);
    color: #fff;
  }

  .b-send {
    margin-top: calc(var(--u) * 4);
  }

  .bico {
    display: block;
    width: calc(var(--u) * 16);
    height: calc(var(--u) * 16);
  }

  /* ── The key card ── */
  .kcard {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border: 1px solid var(--ap-line);
    border-radius: calc(var(--u) * 10);
    background: var(--ap-tint);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .kchead {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: calc(var(--u) * 9) calc(var(--u) * 12);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ap-line);
  }

  .kctitle {
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 11.5);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--ap-blue);
  }

  .kcstate {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 11);
    color: var(--ap-mut);
  }

  .kcrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 9);
    padding: calc(var(--u) * 10) calc(var(--u) * 12) calc(var(--u) * 4);
  }

  .kcwallet {
    display: block;
    flex: none;
    width: calc(var(--u) * 15);
    height: calc(var(--u) * 15);
    color: var(--ap-blue);
  }

  .kcval {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 12);
    color: var(--ap-ink);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .kcopy {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex: none;
    align-items: center;
    gap: calc(var(--u) * 5);
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 12);
    color: var(--ap-ink);
  }

  /* The two glyphs share one cell so the swap cannot move the label beside
   * them - the same construction the page's own copy buttons use. */
  .kico {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    display: block;
    width: calc(var(--u) * 14);
    height: calc(var(--u) * 14);
  }

  .kcopy {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto auto;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .kcopy-l {
    grid-column: 2;
  }

  /* :global() - every glyph here arrives through set:html and never carries
   * the scope attribute a plain selector would demand. */
  .bico :global(svg),
  .kico :global(svg),
  .kcwallet :global(svg) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .kt {
    opacity: 0;
    color: #16a34a;
  }

  .kcnote {
    padding: 0 calc(var(--u) * 12) calc(var(--u) * 10);
    font-size: calc(var(--u) * 11);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ap-mut);
  }

  /* ── Click rings ──
   * A press is not legible from the cursor alone - something has to happen
   * WHERE the cursor is, and a ring that expands and fades is the smallest
   * thing that says "that landed".
   *
   * Each ring lives INSIDE the control it marks rather than at a coordinate on
   * the scene. Hand-written percentages were tried first and were 15 points
   * off the real targets - and would have gone off again the first time the
   * window's padding changed. Anchored, they cannot drift. */
  .ring {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    width: calc(var(--u) * 34);
    height: calc(var(--u) * 34);
    margin: calc(var(--u) * -17) 0 0 calc(var(--u) * -17);
    border: calc(var(--u) * 1.5) solid var(--ap-navy);
    border-radius: 50%;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* ── The cursor ──
   * The track is the WHOLE scene, so translating it by 40% moves the cursor
   * 40% of the scene's width. That is what lets the path below be written in
   * plain percentages without any element knowing its own size.
   *
   * The three stops in `ap-path` are the MEASURED centres of the three
   * targets. The rings anchor themselves; a path cannot, so these stay
   * hand-held - if the window moves or its padding changes, re-measure against
   * the rendered scene rather than reasoning it out. */
  .cur-track {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* Settled state is no cursor at all - a pointer frozen mid-air reads as a
     * broken image. */
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .cur-press {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
  }

  /* ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * The loop. Everything above draws the finished state; everything here is
   * the 9 seconds that earn it.
   * ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
